window.HU_MARKING={"built":"2026-10-22","problems":[{"id":"kmo-2","title":"Eleven red points and a rectangle","source":"Kevin's Mathematical Olympiad, Problem 2","topic":"combinatorics","difficulty":4,"max":10,"reviewed":false,"problem":"Let $M$ be the set of points $(x, y)$ in the Cartesian plane such that $x \\in \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$ and $y \\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}$. Suppose that $11$ of these points are coloured red. Prove that there must exist four red points which form the vertices of a rectangle with sides parallel to the coordinate axes.","solutions":[{"title":"Counting pairs within columns","text":"The grid has $5$ columns ($x$ fixed), each containing $4$ points. Four red points form an axis-parallel rectangle exactly when two different columns each contain red points in the same two rows.\n\nLet column $i$ contain $n_i$ red points, so $\\sum n_i = 11$. Column $i$ contains $\\binom{n_i}{2}$ pairs of red points, and each pair occupies a pair of rows. Let $S=\\sum_{i=1}^{5}\\binom{n_i}{2}$.\n\nNow $S=\\tfrac12\\left(\\sum n_i^2-\\sum n_i\\right)$. By the QM-AM inequality (or Cauchy-Schwarz), $\\sum n_i^2 \\ge \\tfrac{11^2}{5}=24.2$, so $\\sum n_i^2\\ge 25$ and $S\\ge \\tfrac12(25-11)=7$.\n\nThere are only $\\binom42=6$ pairs of rows. Since $S\\ge 7>6$, two of the red pairs occupy the same pair of rows. They cannot lie in the same column, because the pairs within one column occupy different pairs of rows. So two different columns have red points in the same two rows, and these four points form the rectangle."}],"checkpoints":[{"id":"c1","marks":2,"route":"","text":"Reformulates the goal: a rectangle exists if and only if two different columns contain red points in the same pair of rows."},{"id":"c2","marks":2,"route":"","text":"Counts red pairs within columns, $S=\\sum \\binom{n_i}{2}$, and notes there are $\\binom42=6$ pairs of rows."},{"id":"c3","marks":3,"route":"","text":"Proves $S\\ge 7$ with a valid justification (QM-AM, Cauchy-Schwarz, convexity with the extremal distribution $3,2,2,2,2$ justified, or a smoothing argument)."},{"id":"c4","marks":2,"route":"","text":"Applies pigeonhole to $7$ pairs and $6$ row-pairs, and explains why the two matching pairs lie in different columns."},{"id":"c5","marks":1,"route":"","text":"Concludes that these four red points form an axis-parallel rectangle."}],"caps":[{"cap":5,"text":"The bound $S\\ge 7$ is asserted, or \"the minimum is when the points are spread evenly\" is stated without justification."}],"routes":[],"fatal":["Counting pairs within rows instead of columns. With $4$ rows of $5$ points the bound is $\\sum\\binom{m_j}{2}\\ge 10$ against $\\binom52=10$ pairs of columns, which gives no contradiction. A solution that claims a contradiction here has a false step and earns at most the reformulation marks.","A chain of \"without loss of generality, row 4 has at least 2 red points, so the rest has 9 red points in 15 places...\" where the counting does not follow from the previous step. Each unjustified reduction is a gap; if the chain does not establish the result, the pigeonhole and conclusion checkpoints are not met.","Using pigeonhole on columns only (\"some column has 3 red points\") without connecting it to a second column sharing two rows."],"notEnough":["Exhibiting a colouring of $10$ points with no rectangle shows the result is sharp but earns no marks on its own.","Saying \"by pigeonhole there is a rectangle\" without identifying the pigeons and the holes earns nothing for the pigeonhole checkpoint."],"examples":[{"title":"Complete","mark":10,"comment":"Clear reformulation, the bound is proved with Cauchy-Schwarz, and the solution checks that the two matching pairs come from different columns.","script":"Call the vertical lines $x=1,\\dots,5$ columns. A rectangle with sides parallel to the axes is the same thing as two columns that both have red points in rows $a$ and $b$. Let column $i$ have $n_i$ red points; then $n_1+\\dots+n_5=11$. A column with $n_i$ red points gives $\\binom{n_i}{2}$ pairs, each with its own pair of rows. The total number of pairs is $\\sum \\binom{n_i}{2} = \\frac12(\\sum n_i^2 - 11)$. By Cauchy-Schwarz, $5\\sum n_i^2 \\ge (\\sum n_i)^2=121$, so $\\sum n_i^2\\ge 25$ as it is an integer, and the number of pairs is at least $7$. There are only $\\binom{4}{2}=6$ choices of two rows, so two pairs use the same two rows. Two pairs in one column cannot use the same two rows, so they are in different columns, and the four points make a rectangle."},{"title":"Right method, unproved bound","mark":5,"comment":"The method is the intended one, but \"the pairs are minimised when the points are spread out as evenly as possible\" is exactly the step that needs proof. Capped at 5. Also, the solution never says why the two matching pairs are in different columns.","script":"Let column $i$ have $n_i$ red points. The number of red pairs in columns is $\\sum\\binom{n_i}{2}$. This is smallest when the points are spread out as evenly as possible, i.e. $3,2,2,2,2$, giving $3+1+1+1+1=7$. There are $\\binom42=6$ pairs of rows, so by pigeonhole two pairs use the same rows, and that gives a rectangle."},{"title":"Convincing but counts the wrong way","mark":2,"comment":"The reformulation is correct and earns 2. But the solution counts pairs in rows, where the bound is $10$ against $10$ pairs of columns, and then says pigeonhole forces two pairs to coincide. Ten pairs fit into ten places with no repeat, so pigeonhole gives nothing. The argument proves nothing beyond the reformulation.","script":"A rectangle means two rows which have red points in the same two columns. Row $j$ has $m_j$ red points with $\\sum m_j=11$ over 4 rows. The number of pairs in rows is $\\sum\\binom{m_j}{2}$, which is at least $3+3+3+1=10$ when the distribution is $3,3,3,2$. There are $\\binom52=10$ pairs of columns. So we have at least 10 pairs and only 10 places, so by pigeonhole two pairs coincide and we get a rectangle."}]},{"id":"kmo-1","title":"When is $p^q+q^p$ prime?","source":"Kevin's Mathematical Olympiad, Problem 1","topic":"number theory","difficulty":3,"max":10,"reviewed":false,"problem":"Find all pairs of primes $(p, q)$ such that $p^q + q^p$ is also prime.","solutions":[{"title":"Parity, then modulo 3","text":"If $p$ and $q$ are both odd, then $p^q$ and $q^p$ are both odd, so $p^q+q^p$ is even and greater than $2$, hence not prime. So one of them is $2$; by symmetry let $p=2$.\n\nIf $q=2$ then $2^2+2^2=8$, which is not prime. If $q=3$ then $2^3+3^2=17$, which is prime.\n\nNow let $q>3$ be prime. Then $3 \\nmid q$, so $q^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$. Since $q$ is odd, $2^q = 2\\cdot 4^{(q-1)/2} \\equiv 2 \\pmod 3$. Hence $2^q+q^2 \\equiv 0 \\pmod 3$, and since $2^q+q^2>3$ it is composite.\n\nThe pairs are $(2,3)$ and $(3,2)$."}],"checkpoints":[{"id":"c1","marks":2,"route":"","text":"Shows that if $p$ and $q$ are both odd then $p^q+q^p$ is even and greater than $2$, so one of the primes must be $2$."},{"id":"c2","marks":1,"route":"","text":"Rules out $p=q=2$ (the sum is $8$)."},{"id":"c3","marks":1,"route":"","text":"Finds that $q=3$ works, giving $17$."},{"id":"c4","marks":2,"route":"","text":"Shows $q^2\\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$ for every prime $q>3$."},{"id":"c5","marks":2,"route":"","text":"Shows $2^q\\equiv 2 \\pmod 3$ for odd $q$, with a reason (for example $2^q=2\\cdot 4^{(q-1)/2}$, or $2\\equiv -1$ and $q$ odd)."},{"id":"c6","marks":1,"route":"","text":"Concludes $3 \\mid 2^q+q^2$ and that the sum exceeds $3$, so it is composite for every prime $q>3$."},{"id":"c7","marks":1,"route":"","text":"States the complete answer, both $(2,3)$ and $(3,2)$."}],"caps":[{"cap":4,"text":"The case $q>3$ is handled only by checking examples, with no general argument."},{"cap":8,"text":"Either order of the pair is missing from the final answer and nothing else is wrong."}],"routes":[],"fatal":["Treating the case $q>3$ by listing a few values of $q$ and calling the pattern proved. This earns nothing for the three checkpoints on the case $q>3$.","Claiming $2^q\\equiv 2 \\pmod 3$ for all $q$, which fails for even $q$; acceptable only if the solution has already established that $q$ is odd.","Concluding \"divisible by 3, so not prime\" without noting the sum is larger than $3$ loses that checkpoint."],"notEnough":["Stating the answer $(2,3)$ and $(3,2)$ with checking only earns the two marks for the small cases.","Saying \"one of them must be 2 because otherwise the sum is even\" without noting the even sum exceeds $2$ earns 1 of the 2 marks for the parity step."],"examples":[{"title":"Complete and rigorous","mark":10,"comment":"Every checkpoint is met and the case analysis is complete. Concise and correct.","script":"If both primes are odd, the sum of two odd numbers is even and at least $3^3+3^3=54>2$, so it is not prime. Hence one of the primes is 2. Say $p=2$. If $q=2$ we get $8$, not prime. If $q=3$ we get $8+9=17$, prime. If $q\\ge 5$ is prime then $q\\equiv \\pm1 \\pmod 3$, so $q^2\\equiv 1$. Also $q$ is odd and $2\\equiv -1 \\pmod 3$, so $2^q\\equiv (-1)^q=-1\\equiv 2$. So $2^q+q^2\\equiv 0 \\pmod 3$ and $2^q+q^2>3$, so it is not prime. The answer is $(p,q)=(2,3)$ or $(3,2)$."},{"title":"Right idea, checked rather than proved","mark":4,"comment":"The parity step and the small cases are fine, but the case $q>3$ is \"proved\" by examples. Checking $q=5,7,11$ shows a pattern; it does not show that every larger prime fails. Capped at 4.","script":"The sum of two odd numbers is even, and it is bigger than 2, so one prime has to be 2. Take $p=2$. $q=2$: $4+4=8$, no. $q=3$: $8+9=17$, yes. $q=5$: $32+25=57=3\\times 19$, no. $q=7$: $128+49=177=3\\times 59$, no. $q=11$: $2048+121=2169=3\\times 723$, no. These are always multiples of 3, so the only answers are $(2,3)$ and $(3,2)$."},{"title":"Looks complete, but the key step is wrong","mark":8,"comment":"The structure is right and the conclusion is correct, but the claim $2^q\\equiv 2\\pmod 3$ is justified by \"powers of 2 are always 2 mod 3\", which is false ($2^2=4\\equiv 1$). The fact needed is true only because $q$ is odd, and that is the point of the step. The 2 marks for $2^q\\pmod 3$ are withheld.","script":"One of $p,q$ must be 2, otherwise $p^q+q^p$ is even and larger than 2. Let $p=2$. Then $q=2$ gives 8, which fails, and $q=3$ gives 17, which works. For a prime $q>3$, $q$ is not a multiple of 3, so $q^2\\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$. Powers of 2 are always $2 \\bmod 3$, so $2^q\\equiv 2$. Then $2^q+q^2\\equiv 0\\pmod 3$, and it is bigger than 3, so it is composite. So the pairs are $(2,3)$ and $(3,2)$."}]},{"id":"kmo-3","title":"When is $5^n+5^m$ a sum of two squares?","source":"Kevin's Mathematical Olympiad, Problem 3","topic":"number theory","difficulty":4,"max":10,"reviewed":false,"problem":"Let $m$ and $n$ be positive integers. Prove that the sum $5^n + 5^m$ can be expressed as the sum of two perfect squares if and only if $n-m$ is even.","solutions":[{"title":"Explicit squares, then modulo 8","text":"By symmetry assume $n\\ge m$.\n\nIf $n$ and $m$ are both even, $5^n$ and $5^m$ are themselves perfect squares.\n\nIf both are odd, write $n=2k+1$ and $m=2j+1$ with $k\\ge j$, and let $a=5^{k-j}$. Then\n\\[5^n+5^m = 5^{2j}\\cdot 5\\,(a^2+1).\\]\nSince $(2a+1)^2+(a-2)^2 = 5a^2+5$, this equals\n\\[\\left(5^j(2a+1)\\right)^2+\\left(5^j(a-2)\\right)^2,\\]\na sum of two squares.\n\nConversely, suppose $n$ and $m$ have different parities. Since $5^2\\equiv 1 \\pmod 8$, an even power of $5$ is $1 \\pmod 8$ and an odd power is $5 \\pmod 8$, so $5^n+5^m\\equiv 6 \\pmod 8$. Squares are $0$, $1$ or $4 \\pmod 8$, so a sum of two squares is $0,1,2,4$ or $5 \\pmod 8$, never $6$. Hence $5^n+5^m$ is not a sum of two squares."}],"checkpoints":[{"id":"c1","marks":1,"route":"","text":"Handles the case $n$, $m$ both even: each term is already a square."},{"id":"c2","marks":3,"route":"","text":"Handles the case $n$, $m$ both odd with an explicit and correct representation as a sum of two squares (for example via $5(a^2+1)=(2a+1)^2+(a-2)^2$, or the identity $(p^2+q^2)(r^2+s^2)=(pr-qs)^2+(ps+qr)^2$ with $5=1^2+2^2$)."},{"id":"c3","marks":2,"route":"","text":"Shows that a sum of two squares is never $6 \\pmod 8$, by listing squares modulo $8$."},{"id":"c4","marks":2,"route":"","text":"Shows that when $n$ and $m$ have different parities, $5^n+5^m\\equiv 6 \\pmod 8$."},{"id":"c5","marks":1,"route":"","text":"Organises the proof so that both directions of the \"if and only if\" are clearly proved."},{"id":"c6","marks":1,"route":"","text":"The algebra in the explicit representation is shown, not just asserted, and is correct for every $j \\le k$."}],"caps":[{"cap":6,"text":"Only one direction of the \"if and only if\" is proved."}],"routes":[],"fatal":["Working modulo $4$ instead of modulo $8$. Then $5^n+5^m\\equiv 2 \\pmod 4$, but sums of two squares can be $2 \\pmod 4$ (for example $1+1$), so there is no contradiction. The 4 marks for the \"only if\" direction are not earned.","Proving the both-odd case only for $n=m$ (where $2\\cdot 5^n$ is easy) and claiming the general case follows.","Quoting the two-squares theorem (a number is a sum of two squares if and only if every prime $\\equiv 3 \\pmod 4$ divides it to an even power) without checking the prime factorisation of $5^{n-m}+1$. Used correctly and completely, this is an acceptable alternative for the \"only if\" direction."],"notEnough":["Checking small cases such as $5+25=30$ or $5+125=130=9^2+7^2$ earns nothing on its own.","Stating \"$5$ is a sum of two squares, so products of $5$ are too\" without dealing with the factor $a^2+1$ earns at most 1 of the 3 marks for the both-odd case."],"examples":[{"title":"Complete","mark":10,"comment":"Both directions, an explicit identity that is checked, and the right modulus. Exactly what is wanted.","script":"Assume $n\\ge m$. If both are even, $5^n=(5^{n/2})^2$ and $5^m=(5^{m/2})^2$, done. If both are odd, $n=2k+1$, $m=2j+1$, and $5^n+5^m=25^j\\cdot 5(25^{k-j}+1)$. Put $a=5^{k-j}$; then $5(a^2+1)=5a^2+5=(2a+1)^2+(a-2)^2$, which you can check by expanding: $4a^2+4a+1+a^2-4a+4=5a^2+5$. So $5^n+5^m=(5^j(2a+1))^2+(5^j(a-2))^2$. For the converse, if $n,m$ have different parities then one power of 5 is a square, $\\equiv 1 \\pmod 8$, and the other is $5$ times a square, $\\equiv 5 \\pmod 8$, as odd squares are $1 \\pmod 8$. The sum is $6 \\pmod 8$. Squares mod 8 are $0,1,4$, and no two of these add to $6$. So the sum is not a sum of two squares."},{"title":"One direction only","mark":5,"comment":"The \"if\" direction is complete (both cases, with the algebra shown) and earns 5 marks, but the \"only if\" direction is never attempted, so the organisation mark for proving both directions is not earned either.","script":"We need to show it is a sum of two squares when $n-m$ is even. If $n,m$ are both even, each term is a square. If both are odd, take $n=2k+1$, $m=2j+1$ with $k\\ge j$ and $a=5^{k-j}$. Then $5^n+5^m=5^{2j}\\cdot 5(a^2+1)$ and $5(a^2+1)=(2a+1)^2+(a-2)^2$ because both sides equal $5a^2+5$. So $5^n+5^m=(5^j(2a+1))^2+(5^j(a-2))^2$. This proves the statement."},{"title":"Convincing but the modulus is too weak","mark":5,"comment":"The \"if\" direction is correct and earns 5. The \"only if\" argument works modulo 4 and gets $5^n+5^m\\equiv 2 \\pmod 4$, then claims a sum of two squares cannot be $2 \\pmod 4$. That is false ($1^2+1^2=2$), so the converse is not proved.","script":"If $n$ and $m$ are both even, both terms are squares. If both are odd, with $n=2k+1\\ge m=2j+1$ and $a=5^{k-j}$, we have $5^n+5^m=5^{2j}\\cdot 5(a^2+1)=(5^j(2a+1))^2+(5^j(a-2))^2$, since $(2a+1)^2+(a-2)^2=5a^2+5$. Now suppose one exponent is even and the other odd. Every power of 5 is $1 \\pmod 4$, so the sum is $2 \\pmod 4$. A square is $0$ or $1 \\pmod 4$, and a sum of two squares is therefore $0$ or $1 \\pmod 4$, never $2$. So the sum is not a sum of two squares when $n-m$ is odd."}]},{"id":"iran-2012-residue-polynomials","title":"Residue polynomials and complete residue systems","source":"Iran Mathematical Olympiad 2011-2012","topic":"number theory","difficulty":8,"max":10,"reviewed":false,"problem":"Let $p$ be an odd prime. A polynomial $f(x) = \\sum_{j=0}^{n} a_j x^j$ with integer coefficients is called an $i$-residue polynomial if\n\\[ \\sum_{\\substack{p-1 \\mid j \\\\ j > 0}} a_j \\equiv i \\pmod{p}. \\]\n\nProve that the set $\\{f(0), f(1), \\dots, f(p-1)\\}$ forms a complete residue system modulo $p$ if and only if the polynomials $f(x), f^2(x), \\dots, f^{p-2}(x)$ are $0$-residue polynomials and $f^{p-1}(x)$ is a $1$-residue polynomial.\n\nHere $f^k(x)$ means the power $f(x)^k$.","solutions":[{"title":"Power sums, then Newton's identities","text":"Lemma. For an integer $k \\ge 1$,\n\\[ \\sum_{x=0}^{p-1} x^k \\equiv \\begin{cases} -1 \\pmod p, & (p-1) \\mid k, \\\\ 0 \\pmod p, & (p-1) \\nmid k. \\end{cases} \\]\nIf $(p-1)\\mid k$, Fermat's little theorem gives $x^k\\equiv 1$ for $1\\le x\\le p-1$, and $0^k=0$, so the sum is $p-1\\equiv -1$. If $(p-1)\\nmid k$, let $g$ be a primitive root modulo $p$. Then $g^k\\not\\equiv 1$, and $\\sum_{x=1}^{p-1}x^k\\equiv\\sum_{t=0}^{p-2}g^{tk}=\\frac{g^{k(p-1)}-1}{g^k-1}\\equiv 0$, since the numerator is divisible by $p$ and the denominator is not.\n\nReduction. For any polynomial $h(x)=\\sum_j c_j x^j$ with integer coefficients,\n\\[ \\sum_{x=0}^{p-1} h(x)=\\sum_j c_j \\sum_{x=0}^{p-1} x^j \\equiv -\\sum_{\\substack{p-1\\mid j\\\\ j>0}} c_j \\pmod p, \\]\nbecause the $j=0$ term contributes $c_0\\cdot p\\equiv 0$. So $h$ is an $i$-residue polynomial exactly when $\\sum_{x} h(x)\\equiv -i$. Writing $s_k=\\sum_{x=0}^{p-1} f(x)^k$, the condition in the problem is: $s_k\\equiv 0$ for $1\\le k\\le p-2$ and $s_{p-1}\\equiv -1$.\n\nForward direction. If $f(0),\\dots,f(p-1)$ is a complete residue system, then $s_k\\equiv\\sum_{y=0}^{p-1}y^k$, which by the lemma is $0$ for $1\\le k\\le p-2$ and $-1$ for $k=p-1$.\n\nConverse. Let $b_x=f(x)$ and $G(t)=\\prod_{x=0}^{p-1}(t-b_x)=t^p+e_1t^{p-1}+\\dots+e_{p-1}t+e_p$. If no $b_x$ were divisible by $p$, Fermat would give $s_{p-1}\\equiv p\\equiv 0$, contradicting $s_{p-1}\\equiv -1$. So some $b_x\\equiv 0$, and $e_p=\\pm\\prod b_x\\equiv 0$.\nNewton's identities over the integers give $s_k+e_1s_{k-1}+\\dots+e_{k-1}s_1+ke_k=0$ for $1\\le k\\le p-1$. For $k\\le p-2$ the power sums $s_1,\\dots,s_k$ vanish modulo $p$, so $ke_k\\equiv 0$, and $k$ is invertible modulo $p$, giving $e_k\\equiv 0$. For $k=p-1$ this leaves $s_{p-1}+(p-1)e_{p-1}\\equiv 0$, so $-1-e_{p-1}\\equiv 0$ and $e_{p-1}\\equiv -1$.\nHence $G(t)\\equiv t^p-t\\pmod p$. Every residue $c$ satisfies $c^p-c\\equiv 0$, so $\\prod_x (c-b_x)\\equiv 0$, and since $p$ is prime some $b_x\\equiv c$. The $p$ values $b_x$ meet all $p$ residues, so each exactly once: they form a complete residue system."},{"title":"Power sums, then counting with an indicator","text":"The lemma, the reduction and the forward direction are as in the first solution. For the converse, fix a residue $c$ and let $N_c$ be the number of $x\\in\\{0,\\dots,p-1\\}$ with $f(x)\\equiv c$. By Fermat, $1-(y-c)^{p-1}\\equiv 1$ if $y\\equiv c$ and $\\equiv 0$ otherwise, so\n\\[ N_c\\equiv\\sum_{x=0}^{p-1}\\bigl(1-(f(x)-c)^{p-1}\\bigr)\\equiv -\\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\\binom{p-1}{k}(-c)^{p-1-k}s_k \\pmod p, \\]\nusing $\\sum_x 1=p\\equiv 0$. Here $s_0=p\\equiv 0$, $s_k\\equiv 0$ for $1\\le k\\le p-2$ and $s_{p-1}\\equiv -1$, so only the $k=p-1$ term survives and $N_c\\equiv 1\\pmod p$.\nSince $0\\le N_c\\le p$, this forces $N_c=1$ for every $c$ (the only alternative, $N_c=p+1$, is too big). So every residue is taken exactly once."}],"checkpoints":[{"id":"c1","marks":2,"route":"","text":"Proves the power-sum lemma: $\\sum_{x=0}^{p-1}x^k$ is $-1$ modulo $p$ when $(p-1)\\mid k$ and $0$ otherwise, for $k\\ge 1$ (a primitive root, or multiplying by an $a$ with $a^k\\not\\equiv 1$). Stating it as a well-known result without proof earns 1."},{"id":"c2","marks":1,"route":"","text":"Deduces $\\sum_{x}h(x)\\equiv -\\sum_{(p-1)\\mid j,\\ j>0}c_j$ for any integer polynomial $h$, and so rewrites the conditions as $s_k\\equiv 0$ for $1\\le k\\le p-2$ and $s_{p-1}\\equiv -1$, where $s_k=\\sum_x f(x)^k$."},{"id":"c3","marks":1,"route":"","text":"Proves the forward direction: a complete residue system has the same power sums as $0,1,\\dots,p-1$, then applies the lemma."},{"id":"c4","marks":1,"route":"A","text":"Shows some $f(x)$ is divisible by $p$ (otherwise $s_{p-1}\\equiv p\\equiv 0$), so the constant term of $\\prod(t-f(x))$ is $0$ modulo $p$."},{"id":"c5","marks":2,"route":"A","text":"Uses Newton's identities to get $e_1\\equiv\\dots\\equiv e_{p-2}\\equiv 0$, noting that $k$ is invertible modulo $p$ for $k\\le p-2$."},{"id":"c6","marks":1,"route":"A","text":"Uses the $k=p-1$ identity to find $e_{p-1}\\equiv -1$, so $\\prod(t-f(x))\\equiv t^p-t$."},{"id":"c7","marks":2,"route":"A","text":"Concludes correctly from $\\prod(t-f(x))\\equiv t^p-t$: each residue is a root, $p$ prime gives some $f(x)\\equiv c$, and $p$ values covering $p$ residues means each appears once."},{"id":"c8","marks":2,"route":"B","text":"Expresses $N_c$, the number of $x$ with $f(x)\\equiv c$, modulo $p$ as $\\sum_x\\bigl(1-(f(x)-c)^{p-1}\\bigr)$ using Fermat."},{"id":"c9","marks":2,"route":"B","text":"Expands binomially and uses the power-sum conditions, including $s_0=p\\equiv 0$, to get $N_c\\equiv 1 \\pmod p$."},{"id":"c10","marks":2,"route":"B","text":"Concludes $N_c=1$ for every $c$ from $0\\le N_c\\le p$."}],"caps":[{"cap":4,"text":"Only the forward direction (a complete residue system satisfies the conditions) is proved."}],"routes":["A","B"],"fatal":["Claiming that the power sums $s_1,\\dots,s_{p-1}$ determine the multiset of values modulo $p$ \"by Newton's identities\" without dealing with $e_p$. The identity at $k=p$ reads $pe_p\\equiv\\dots$, which says nothing about $e_p$ modulo $p$. A converse resting on this claim earns none of the Route A marks it skips.","Deriving $e_p\\equiv 0$ from Newton's identity at $k=p$, or dividing by $k=p$. The Route A constant-term checkpoint is not earned.","Reading $f^k(x)$ as the composition $f(f(\\cdots f(x)))$ rather than the power $f(x)^k$. This solves a different problem; only the lemma can be credited.","Writing $\\sum_x\\sum_j a_jx^j=\\bigl(\\sum_j a_j\\bigr)\\bigl(\\sum_x x^j\\bigr)$. This is false as written; if the next line makes the intended $\\sum_j a_j\\sum_x x^j$ clear, treat it as a minor slip costing nothing, otherwise the reduction checkpoint is not earned.","In Route B, forgetting that $N_c$ could be $p+1$ in principle, or concluding $N_c=1$ from $N_c\\equiv 1$ without the bound, loses 1 of the 2 conclusion marks."],"notEnough":["Checking the statement for $p=3$ or $p=5$, or for particular polynomials such as $f(x)=x$, earns nothing.","Stating Newton's identities without applying them modulo $p$ earns nothing towards Route A.","Observing that a complete residue system means $f$ is a bijection on residues, without connecting it to the residue conditions, earns nothing."],"examples":[{"title":"Full marks, counting argument","mark":10,"comment":"Complete and efficient. The lemma is proved, the reduction is clean, and the indicator argument handles the converse with the bound $N_c\\le p$ stated.","script":"First, for $k\\ge1$ let $T_k=\\sum_{x=0}^{p-1}x^k$. If $p-1\\mid k$ then $x^k\\equiv1$ for $x\\ne0$ so $T_k\\equiv p-1\\equiv-1$. If $p-1\\nmid k$, take a primitive root $g$; then $g^k\\not\\equiv1$ and multiplying the nonzero residues by $g$ permutes them, so $g^kT_k\\equiv T_k$, giving $T_k\\equiv0$.\n\nFor any integer polynomial $h(x)=\\sum c_jx^j$, $\\sum_{x=0}^{p-1}h(x)=\\sum_j c_jT_j$ (with $T_0=p\\equiv0$), which is $-\\sum_{p-1\\mid j,\\,j>0}c_j$. So $h$ is $i$-residue iff $\\sum_x h(x)\\equiv -i$. Let $s_k=\\sum_x f(x)^k$. The condition is $s_1\\equiv\\dots\\equiv s_{p-2}\\equiv0$ and $s_{p-1}\\equiv-1$.\n\nIf the values are a complete residue system then $s_k\\equiv T_k$, and the lemma gives exactly this.\n\nConversely, for a residue $c$ let $N_c=\\#\\{x: f(x)\\equiv c\\}$. Since $(y-c)^{p-1}$ is $0$ if $y\\equiv c$ and $1$ otherwise, $N_c\\equiv\\sum_x(1-(f(x)-c)^{p-1})\\equiv-\\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\\binom{p-1}{k}(-c)^{p-1-k}s_k$. All terms vanish except $k=p-1$, so $N_c\\equiv-s_{p-1}\\equiv1$. But $0\\le N_c\\le p$, so $N_c=1$ for all $c$, and the values form a complete residue system."},{"title":"Newton's identities, last step asserted","mark":9,"comment":"A strong Route A solution. The only gap is the end: \"so the $b_x$ are $0,1,\\dots,p-1$\" needs a reason, namely that each residue is a root of $t^p-t$ and $p$ is prime, so some $b_x$ hits it, and then counting. One of the two conclusion marks is withheld.","script":"Lemma: $\\sum_{x=0}^{p-1}x^k\\equiv-1$ if $p-1\\mid k$ and $0$ otherwise. Proof: the first case is Fermat. Otherwise pick $a$ with $a^k\\not\\equiv1$ (a primitive root works); $x\\mapsto ax$ permutes the nonzero residues, so $(a^k-1)\\sum x^k\\equiv0$, hence $\\sum x^k\\equiv0$.\n\nSo for any $h=\\sum c_jx^j$, $\\sum_{x}h(x)\\equiv-\\sum_{p-1\\mid j>0}c_j$. With $s_k=\\sum_x f(x)^k$ the conditions say $s_k\\equiv0$ for $k\\le p-2$ and $s_{p-1}\\equiv-1$. If the $f(x)$ are a complete residue system, $s_k\\equiv\\sum y^k$ and the lemma gives the conditions.\n\nConversely let $b_x=f(x)$ and $\\prod(t-b_x)=t^p+e_1t^{p-1}+\\dots+e_p$. Not all $b_x$ are units, otherwise $s_{p-1}\\equiv p\\equiv0$; so some $b_x\\equiv0$ and $e_p\\equiv0$. Newton: $s_k+e_1s_{k-1}+\\dots+ke_k=0$. For $k\\le p-2$ this gives $ke_k\\equiv0$ and $k$ is invertible, so $e_k\\equiv0$. For $k=p-1$: $-1+(p-1)e_{p-1}\\equiv0$, so $e_{p-1}\\equiv-1$. Hence $\\prod(t-b_x)\\equiv t^p-t=t(t-1)\\cdots(t-p+1)$, so the $b_x$ are $0,1,\\dots,p-1$ in some order."},{"title":"Forward direction and a start on the converse","mark":5,"comment":"The lemma, the reduction and the forward direction are all correct, and the observation that some $f(x)\\equiv0$ is the right first step for Route A. The rest of the converse is \"similarly, by symmetric functions\", which is not an argument. 5 marks.","script":"We use $\\sum_{x=0}^{p-1}x^k\\equiv0$ unless $p-1\\mid k$, when it is $-1$. Proof: if $p-1\\mid k$, Fermat gives $p-1$ ones. If not, with a primitive root $g$ the sum is a geometric series $\\frac{g^{k(p-1)}-1}{g^k-1}\\equiv0$ because $g^k\\ne1$.\n\nThen $\\sum_x f(x)^k$ is minus the sum of the coefficients of $f^k$ in degrees divisible by $p-1$. So $f^k$ is $0$-residue iff $\\sum_x f(x)^k\\equiv0$ and $1$-residue iff it is $\\equiv-1$.\n\nIf $f(0),\\dots,f(p-1)$ is complete, $\\sum_x f(x)^k\\equiv\\sum_y y^k$, which is $0$ for $k\\le p-2$ and $-1$ for $k=p-1$. Done.\n\nFor the converse, if none of the $f(x)$ were $0$ mod $p$ then $\\sum f(x)^{p-1}\\equiv p\\equiv0\\ne-1$, so some $f(x)\\equiv0$. Similarly, using symmetric functions, all the other residues must appear, so the set is complete."},{"title":"Convincing but the key step is false","mark":4,"comment":"Everything up to the converse is right and earns 4. The converse claims that the power sums $s_1,\\dots,s_{p-1}$ determine the polynomial $\\prod(t-f(x))$ modulo $p$. Newton's identities only give $e_1,\\dots,e_{p-1}$; the identity for $e_p$ has the coefficient $p\\equiv0$, so the constant term is not determined, and the argument never shows it is $0$. No Route A marks are earned.","script":"Lemma: for $k\\ge1$, $\\sum_{x=0}^{p-1}x^k\\equiv-1$ if $p-1\\mid k$, else $0$. If $p-1\\mid k$ this is Fermat. If not, let $g$ be a primitive root; the nonzero residues are $g^0,\\dots,g^{p-2}$ and the sum is $\\frac{g^{k(p-1)}-1}{g^k-1}\\equiv0$.\n\nHence for a polynomial $h$, $\\sum_x h(x)\\equiv-$(sum of coefficients of $x^j$ with $p-1\\mid j$, $j>0$). So the conditions say that the numbers $b_x=f(x)$ have power sums $s_k\\equiv0$ for $1\\le k\\le p-2$ and $s_{p-1}\\equiv-1$, which are exactly the power sums of $0,1,\\dots,p-1$. This proves the forward direction.\n\nFor the converse: by Newton's identities, the power sums of a set of numbers determine its elementary symmetric polynomials, and hence the polynomial with those numbers as roots. The $b_x$ and $0,1,\\dots,p-1$ have the same power sums, so $\\prod(t-b_x)\\equiv\\prod(t-c)$ and the $b_x$ are a permutation of $0,\\dots,p-1$."},{"title":"Examples instead of a proof","mark":1,"comment":"The lemma is quoted correctly but not proved, which earns 1. The rest checks $p=3$ and one polynomial, which proves nothing about general $p$ or $f$.","script":"We know that $1^k+2^k+\\dots+(p-1)^k$ is $0$ mod $p$ unless $p-1$ divides $k$, when it is $-1$.\n\nTake $p=3$ and $f(x)=x$. Then $f(0),f(1),f(2)=0,1,2$ is complete. $f$ is $x$, and the only $j$ with $2\\mid j$, $j>0$ and $a_j\\ne0$ would be even powers, so $f$ is $0$-residue. $f^2=x^2$ has $a_2=1$, so it is $1$-residue. This matches the statement. Also for $f(x)=x^2$ the values are $0,1,1$, not complete, and $f^1=x^2$ has $a_2=1\\ne0$, so it is not $0$-residue, which also matches. So the statement is true."}]}]};
